From 1ad64a5d2b964ebdb27c0832da498682f91e9fc4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sbase25 <sbaselmans1@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 16:52:08 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] seccomp: allow legacy syscalls for Samsung codec2 service
 on GSIs

Samsung's Android 11 samsung.software.media.c2 HAL ships a seccomp
policy that predates syscalls used by newer bionic (e.g. rseq). On an
Android 16 GSI the service is SIGSYS-killed at startup, which makes
mediaserver hang waiting for IComponentStore; StorageManagerService then
blocks on codec enumeration and /sdcard never mounts (camera, ringtones
and Settings->Storage all break).

Narrowly scope a workaround to that one process (comm starts with
"samsung.softwar"): log the otherwise-fatal syscall and allow it
instead of killing. All other processes keep full seccomp enforcement.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KfdEw7FqSm92uoQmEa9GaX
---
 kernel/seccomp.c | 14 ++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+)

diff --git a/kernel/seccomp.c b/kernel/seccomp.c
index b56f574e6..63128e822 100755
--- a/kernel/seccomp.c
+++ b/kernel/seccomp.c
@@ -674,6 +674,20 @@ static int __seccomp_filter(int this_syscall, const struct seccomp_data *sd,
 	data = filter_ret & SECCOMP_RET_DATA;
 	action = filter_ret & SECCOMP_RET_ACTION_FULL;
 
+	/*
+	 * touchGrass: Samsung's Android 11 codec2 service ships a seccomp
+	 * policy that predates syscalls used by newer bionic (e.g. rseq),
+	 * so on Android 16 GSIs it is killed at startup.  Only for that
+	 * process, log the blocked syscall and allow it instead of killing.
+	 */
+	if ((action == SECCOMP_RET_TRAP || action == SECCOMP_RET_KILL_THREAD ||
+	     action == SECCOMP_RET_KILL_PROCESS) && current->group_leader &&
+	    !strncmp(current->group_leader->comm, "samsung.softwar", 15)) {
+		pr_info_ratelimited("touchGrass: seccomp allow syscall %d for %s\n",
+				    this_syscall, current->group_leader->comm);
+		action = SECCOMP_RET_ALLOW;
+	}
+
 	switch (action) {
 	case SECCOMP_RET_ERRNO:
 		/* Set low-order bits as an errno, capped at MAX_ERRNO. */
